<div class="faq-section">
  <!-- Student Q&A --> <h2>Student Q&A: Mass Communication After PUC</h2>
  
<details class="faq-item"> <summary>What exams should I write after PUC for mass communication in Karnataka?</summary> <div class="faq-answer"> <p>
For BJMC/BMM programs, most universities conduct their own entrance exams or admit
              based on 12th marks. Some private colleges accept direct admission. For film schools
              like FTII, write their specific entrance exam. For digital media careers, focus on
              building a portfolio rather than entrance exams.
</p> </div> </details>
<details class="faq-item"> <summary>Is a mass communication degree necessary for media careers?</summary> <div class="faq-answer"> <p>
No. While degrees offer structure and credibility, many successful media professionals
              are self-taught or certification-based. Your portfolio, skills, and network matter
              more after the first job. Focus on building a strong body of work, not just chasing a
              degree.
</p> </div> </details>
<details class="faq-item"> <summary>What's the difference between BJMC and BMM?</summary> <div class="faq-answer"> <p>
BJMC (Bachelor of Journalism & Mass Communication) focuses more on journalism, news
              media, and traditional reporting. BMM (Bachelor of Mass Media) is broader, covering
              advertising, PR, digital media, and film. BJMC is more specialized; BMM offers more
              flexibility.
</p> </div> </details>
<details class="faq-item"> <summary>Can I switch from engineering/medical to media later?</summary> <div class="faq-answer"> <p>
Yes. Many professionals transition to media after engineering/medical degrees. You can
              pursue a master's in media, build a portfolio through freelance work, or start a
              media-focused side project. However, it requires dedication and portfolio building -
              weigh the time investment.
</p> </div> </details>
<details class="faq-item"> <summary>What if I want to work abroad after media studies?</summary> <div class="faq-answer"> <p>
To work abroad in media, you typically need: (1) Strong portfolio demonstrating your
              skills, (2) Excellent English communication skills, (3) Understanding of global media
              trends, (4) Willingness to relocate. Start with local internships to build experience,
              then apply to global opportunities. Research country-specific visa/work permit
              requirements early.
</p> </div> </details>
</div>









